David Corbett wrote:
> In real life, I wouldn't forget whether or not I'm wearing gloves, but I 
> don't expect NetHack to prompt me before picking up a cockatrice corpse, 
> either.

Case 1: Knowing the consequences of an action requires special knowledge
only obtainable through experience or spoiling.  In this case, prompting
the player is itself a "spoil," since the character could legitimately
not know the consequences of the action.  E.g., someone doesn't know
that touching a footrice will cause stoning, asking to confirm gives
this knowledge away.  Or, stepping into a pool of water without
levitation or water walking boots causes potions to become diluted
or scrolls to become blank.  Or putting a wand of cancellation/bag
of holding/bag of tricks into a bag of holding.

Such things are part of the learning curve and add richness and texture
to the game.  Some conducts clearly belong here as well, such as
vegetarianism, since you have to know what is/is not legal food
for maintaining conduct.  Prompting here would spoil that knowledge.

I also think it makes perfect sense that getting infected with
lycanthropy and changing form/eating a chameleon/stepping on a
polymorph trap breaks changelessness without prompting since knowing
the consequences of these actions all require special knowledge.

Case 2: Knowing the consequences of an action requires special knowledge
only obtainable through experience or spoiling, but the game gives it
away anyway by prompting.  E.g., Stanislav's example of applying a
wand and the resulting prompt about breaking the wand, or when you
go up on level 1 and the game prompts you to be sure because there
is no turning back.  Or when you are a wizard and the game asks
you if you want to continue reading a difficult spellbook.

I don't think there are many of these, and I'm glad.  I'd get rid
of the leaving level 1 prompt, personally.  The main justification
for the apply/break prompt is, as Stanislav said, because there
is no separate break command.  The wizard/spellbook thing is
just too cute to cut out ;)

Case 3: Knowing the consequences of an action requires no special
knowledge and the game prompts you anyway.  E.g., when you accidentally
run into a peaceful monster without stormbringer and the game
prompts you as to whether you really want to strike the monster.
Or when you #pray and the game asks if you really want to pray.

Personally, I'd put the conducts that don't require special knowledge
here (illiteracy, athiesm, weaponlessness, wishlessness, nudism,
taking off your blindfold in a Zen game, etc.).  Note that you only
need to ask until the conduct has been broken.  For most players,
this means you ask the question at most once per game.

Case 4: Knowing the consequences of an action requires no special
knowledge and the game doesn't prompt you: virtually everything :)

I don't really expect to convince anyone, as TDTToE.  We all
know Nethack is perfect exactly as it is and there's no need to
ever go past 3.4.3 :)


